  "full_prompt": "Create an animation explaining the paradox of the derivative. Start with a car traveling from A to B, showing distance vs time graph s(t) and velocity vs time graph v(t). Explain that velocity requires two separate points in time to compute (change in distance / change in time), but the derivative associates a velocity with a single instant. Show the speedometer measuring velocity over a small time interval dt with distance change ds. Demonstrate that ds/dt is the slope between two close points on the graph. Then show that as dt approaches zero, the secant line approaches the tangent line, and the derivative is the slope of the tangent line. Use the specific example s(t) = t³ to show the algebra: expanding (t+dt)³, canceling terms, and taking the limit dt → 0 to get 3t². Finally, address the paradox at t=0: derivative is 0, but the car does move over any finite interval. Conclude that the derivative is the best constant approximation for rate of change, not an instantaneous rate of change.",
